WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.080
- Welcome back y'all.

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:04.413
I'm here with the cast of
Book Club The Next Chapter,

00:00:04.413 --> 00:00:06.357
with Jane Fonda, Candice Bergen,

00:00:06.357 --> 00:00:08.280
Diane Keaton and Mary Steenburgen.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:09.656
(audience cheers)

00:00:09.656 --> 00:00:12.180
So we're gonna go through
some of our personal

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:13.680
book club picks in a moment.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:17.190
But first, do y'all prefer
fiction or non-fiction?

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:18.270
Like what's your preference usually?

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:19.170
- Non-fiction.

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:20.038
- Non-fiction?

00:00:20.038 --> 00:00:20.871
- Yeah.

00:00:20.871 --> 00:00:21.704
- Yeah.

00:00:21.704 --> 00:00:22.537
- Yeah. I just feel life is so short

00:00:22.537 --> 00:00:23.520
and there's so much to learn.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:25.410
So, I'm non-fiction gal.

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:26.243
- What about you, Candice?

00:00:26.243 --> 00:00:27.360
- I agree non-fiction.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:28.193
- [Kelly] Okay.

00:00:28.193 --> 00:00:31.650
- But Mary put me onto a
fiction book that I loved.

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:33.000
What was it? A Russian-

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.086
- Oh, A Gentleman in
Moscow by Amor Towles.

00:00:35.086 --> 00:00:36.420
- [Candice] A Gentleman in Moscow.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:37.980
- Yeah, it was a great read.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:39.060
- [Mary] I love that book

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.250
and I actually got to meet him

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.650
'cause I kind of fangirled out and

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:44.483
- [Kelly] I love that.

00:00:44.483 --> 00:00:45.316
- wanted to meet him

00:00:45.316 --> 00:00:46.290
and he's wonderful.

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:47.370
Yeah, he's a great writer.

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:48.720
- So do you generally gravitate

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:50.430
towards fiction or non-fiction?

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:52.920
- Well, I read non-fiction because

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:56.010
Jane sends me books and
tells me this is good

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:57.783
and you should read this.

00:00:57.783 --> 00:01:00.210
So I'm trying to keep up with
the Jane Fonda reading list

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:02.790
but when I go buy books,
it's usually fiction.

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:03.623
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:01:03.623 --> 00:01:04.950
- I love stories, you know.

00:01:04.950 --> 00:01:06.750
- I love escapism in that sense too.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:08.010
It's nice, yeah.

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:08.843
What about you, Diane?

00:01:08.843 --> 00:01:10.290
What do you generally float towards?

00:01:10.290 --> 00:01:11.123
- Yeah, Diane.

00:01:11.123 --> 00:01:12.841
- That's Diane, that's
you, that's your name.

00:01:12.841 --> 00:01:13.674
- [Mary] She's busy writing them.

00:01:13.674 --> 00:01:14.507
- No, no.

00:01:14.507 --> 00:01:15.990
I love visuals.

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:18.960
I like books with like,
lots of beautiful images.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:20.670
- [Kelly] Oh.
- And I'm addicted to that

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:22.620
and I have a lot of it.

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:24.000
So if you wanna come to my house

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.123
and look at my pictures.
- Oh, say yes to that.

00:01:26.123 --> 00:01:28.320
- You know her library's black and white.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:29.153
- [Kelly] That's, so-

00:01:29.153 --> 00:01:30.540
- All the books are either
black and white, right?

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:31.373
- Oh really?

00:01:31.373 --> 00:01:32.206
Like you rewrap them?

00:01:32.206 --> 00:01:33.039
- They're not all black or white, no.

00:01:33.039 --> 00:01:33.886
I'm not quite that bad,

00:01:33.886 --> 00:01:34.719
but I like it.

00:01:34.719 --> 00:01:35.615
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:35.615 --> 00:01:37.020
- I, it's so true.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:38.280
I worked with this photographer,

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:39.113
Brian Bowen Smith,

00:01:39.113 --> 00:01:40.350
and he had a book

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:43.440
and literally the
pictures tell such a story

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:44.580
and it's so cool.

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:46.110
And how you capture like,

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:47.820
people like the story in their eyes,

00:01:47.820 --> 00:01:48.653
like their movement,

00:01:48.653 --> 00:01:50.610
anything like slight,
like that is so moving.

00:01:50.610 --> 00:01:51.443
I agree.

00:01:51.443 --> 00:01:52.590
That's- nobody usually answers with that.

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:53.670
That's nice.

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:55.140
Well, we asked you before the show

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:56.970
about books that you'd pick

00:01:56.970 --> 00:01:58.620
as your own book club selection.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:01:59.700
So let's start with Mary.

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:00.900
Mary, what's, you picked

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:01.980
A Gentleman in Moscow.

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:03.690
- [Mary] A Gentleman in Moscow.

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:06.450
I also, if I go back

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:09.330
I loved books like Cry,
the Beloved Country

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:12.216
and I was glad to be able
to read that in school.

00:02:12.216 --> 00:02:13.230
- That's awesome.

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:14.063
Okay, I love that book.

00:02:14.063 --> 00:02:14.896
What about you Diane?

00:02:14.896 --> 00:02:16.620
What did you choose?
- You just did, you did me.

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:17.854
Move on.

00:02:17.854 --> 00:02:18.687
(Kelly laughs)

00:02:18.687 --> 00:02:19.980
(audience laughs)

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:21.300
- Did you not pick a book?

00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:22.290
You picked a picture one.

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:23.572
Okay, okay, okay.

00:02:23.572 --> 00:02:24.405
- Yeah, that's okay.

00:02:24.405 --> 00:02:26.434
- I'm not sure that counts.

00:02:26.434 --> 00:02:28.023
- Well, if it doesn't count, it's okay.

00:02:28.023 --> 00:02:29.040
- [Kelly] It's okay.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:29.873
We'll move on.

00:02:29.873 --> 00:02:30.990
Candice, what about you?

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:35.430
- Me, I would pick Passage to India.

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:36.810
- Okay. Right on.

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:37.860
Why do you love that so much?

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:38.693
Why would you recommend?

00:02:38.693 --> 00:02:42.390
- Because I love the feeling
of the dust and the schmutz

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:47.010
and the, and that you
get to know India and

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:49.140
and these incredible characters

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:52.440
and that it's beautifully written and um-

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:53.273
- Seems descriptive.

00:02:53.273 --> 00:02:54.506
Yeah.

00:02:54.506 --> 00:02:55.339
That's nice.

00:02:55.339 --> 00:02:57.005
What about you? Like,
what's your book, you pick?

00:02:57.005 --> 00:02:58.823
- Well it so happens. I like
the book I wrote mostly.

00:02:58.823 --> 00:03:00.231
- Love you.

00:03:00.231 --> 00:03:01.316
(Kelly laughs)

00:03:01.316 --> 00:03:02.149
- I've written a number of
books. (audience cheers)

00:03:02.149 --> 00:03:03.815
- [Kelly] I love it. Oh,
this is an important one.

00:03:03.815 --> 00:03:04.648
- Yeah.

00:03:04.648 --> 00:03:05.481
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:05.481 --> 00:03:06.498
What can I do?

00:03:06.498 --> 00:03:07.331
The path

00:03:07.331 --> 00:03:08.610
- [Jane] I was very depressed
because what's happening

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:10.500
to the planet with the climate crisis.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:11.333
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:11.333 --> 00:03:12.600
And so I went to DC

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:14.995
and got arrested a bunch of times.

00:03:14.995 --> 00:03:16.254
- [Kelly] Good for you.

00:03:16.254 --> 00:03:17.320
Good for you.

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:18.780
(audience cheers)

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:21.477
- Turned 82 in jail and um

00:03:21.477 --> 00:03:22.310
- Did you really?

00:03:22.310 --> 00:03:23.143
- [Jane] Yeah.

00:03:23.143 --> 00:03:24.191
- You're my hero.

00:03:24.191 --> 00:03:26.160
- I figured that if I
could turn 82 in jail

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:27.420
a lot of people would say, well God

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:29.682
if she could do it, I'm
gonna, I'm gonna try.

00:03:29.682 --> 00:03:32.250
We've gotta move from
being concerned about

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:34.440
climate to taking action about climate.

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:35.700
We're running out of time.

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:36.816
And so I wrote this book.

00:03:36.816 --> 00:03:37.649
(audience claps)

00:03:37.649 --> 00:03:39.157
- [Kelly] I love It.

00:03:39.157 --> 00:03:42.570
- Describing how, you know,
hope is so interesting.

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:44.220
It's very different than optimism.

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:47.160
Optimism's everything's gonna be okay,

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.950
but you don't do anything to make it so.

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:53.730
Hope is like action with
its sleeve rolled up.

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:55.059
- [Kelly] Powerful.

00:03:55.059 --> 00:03:56.965
- Hope is, you know, don't you

00:03:56.965 --> 00:03:58.140
Greta Thunberg said something great.

00:03:58.140 --> 00:03:59.160
Don't go looking for hope.

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.530
Look for action and hope will be there.

00:04:01.530 --> 00:04:03.390
And that's what this book is about.

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:04.223
- Wow.

00:04:04.223 --> 00:04:05.056
What a great line.

00:04:05.056 --> 00:04:05.889
Yeah.

00:04:05.889 --> 00:04:06.780
- It's called, it's called

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:11.100
What Can I Do? My Path From
Climate Despair to Action.

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:12.211
I encourage everyone

00:04:12.211 --> 00:04:13.044
- And it's brilliant.

00:04:13.044 --> 00:04:13.877
It's brilliant.

00:04:13.877 --> 00:04:15.090
- It's very good, easy read

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:17.070
and full of information about what to do.

00:04:17.070 --> 00:04:17.903
- All right.

00:04:17.903 --> 00:04:18.736
I like it.

00:04:18.736 --> 00:04:21.388
So Mary, you mentioned
a gentleman in Moscow,

00:04:21.388 --> 00:04:22.221
so what do you love about it though?

00:04:22.221 --> 00:04:23.054
What do you, why did
you recommend the book?

00:04:23.054 --> 00:04:25.440
Why do you recommend it for people?

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:27.690
- It, well, I guess

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:29.220
because of what we do for a living.

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:30.913
I love the creation of characters

00:04:30.913 --> 00:04:35.850
and he just created such
a totally unique character

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:37.170
in a unique world.

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:40.440
And it was a story I'd never
heard anything like it before.

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:41.273
- Fresh.

00:04:41.273 --> 00:04:42.316
Yeah.

00:04:42.316 --> 00:04:43.391
- Very magical book.

00:04:43.391 --> 00:04:45.750
I just did wanna say
about Jane's book that

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:48.870
the thing I love about that
is big and small things,

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:51.420
including things nobody
had ever said before

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:52.680
that you can do for the climate.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:55.290
Like in terms of what you invest in

00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:57.390
and what you know and banking

00:04:57.390 --> 00:04:58.650
and everything else.

00:04:58.650 --> 00:05:00.600
It's such a brilliant book

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.790
about what you as a person can do.

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:03.623
- Thank you, Mary.

00:05:03.623 --> 00:05:04.456
- [Mary] It's true.

00:05:04.456 --> 00:05:05.673
- [Candace] Yeah.

00:05:05.673 --> 00:05:06.506
- It's helpful.

00:05:06.506 --> 00:05:07.339
That's what's great about it.

00:05:07.339 --> 00:05:08.172
All right, everybody.

00:05:08.172 --> 00:05:10.807
Book Club: The Next Chapter
is in theaters on May 12th

00:05:10.807 --> 00:05:11.796
- Directors right there.

00:05:11.796 --> 00:05:13.305
- Directors right there.

00:05:13.305 --> 00:05:14.138
(audience cheers)

00:05:14.138 --> 00:05:17.016
(Kelly makes alarm noise)

00:05:17.016 --> 00:05:19.050
And Focus Teachers is giving everyone

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:21.570
in the audience a pair of
tickets to go see the movie.

00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:23.340
(audience cheers)

00:05:23.340 --> 00:05:24.480
So, like I said earlier

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:26.340
last year I launched a new initiative.

00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:28.638
It's called Stranger Reads.

00:05:28.638 --> 00:05:29.932
(upbeat music)

00:05:29.932 --> 00:05:32.910
♪ Stranger Reads ♪

00:05:32.910 --> 00:05:35.010
We're inviting everyone to step

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:36.450
outside our reading preferences

00:05:36.450 --> 00:05:38.970
and discover books we'd
otherwise never pick up.

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:41.940
Often these book recommendations
come from total strangers.

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:43.560
It's happened to me multiple times

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:45.803
and I've loved every book.
Has this ever happened to you?

00:05:45.803 --> 00:05:46.920
Have you ever seen
somebody reading something

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:47.753
and they're so into it

00:05:47.753 --> 00:05:49.110
and you're like, what are you reading?

00:05:49.110 --> 00:05:49.943
I've done that a lot.

00:05:49.943 --> 00:05:50.776
Have you never done that?

00:05:50.776 --> 00:05:52.014
- Yes.

00:05:52.014 --> 00:05:52.847
- Okay.

00:05:52.847 --> 00:05:53.680
Thank you.

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:55.560
Thank you, Candice. The last
time we did Stranger Reads,

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:57.090
my recommendation came from

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:58.920
Nobel Prize winning humanitarian,

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:01.830
Malala Yousafzai of all people.

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:02.663
It was incredible.

00:06:02.663 --> 00:06:03.496
Check this out.

00:06:03.496 --> 00:06:06.780
Malala, I was curious if you
wanted to share a book with me

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:09.420
and I would share one of my
favorites with you today.

00:06:09.420 --> 00:06:11.280
- Of course, I would love
to share a book with you.

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:14.411
Last year, my Literary
book club had this memoir

00:06:14.411 --> 00:06:19.217
called Aftershocks by a
debut author Nadia Owusu.

00:06:19.217 --> 00:06:22.770
And she is abandoned by
her mother at age two

00:06:22.770 --> 00:06:24.420
and orphaned by her, you know,

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:26.550
when her father dies at age 13.

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:28.890
And this is about how
this young woman has to

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:31.680
create her own home and
her own place in the world

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:34.140
and the strength and
courage it takes to do that.

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:35.430
And I think it would really resonate

00:06:35.430 --> 00:06:37.260
with you and all your viewers, Kelly.

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:38.820
So I hope you would enjoy that.

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:39.675
- Oh I love it.

00:06:39.675 --> 00:06:40.620
(audience claps)

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:42.090
She's so cool, y'all.

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:44.289
Now I have another book I wanna recommend.

00:06:44.289 --> 00:06:46.920
It's called Genius of Place.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:50.190
It's the Life of Frederick
Law Olmsted by Justin Martin.

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:51.030
I love this book.

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:52.946
I actually was reading a fiction book

00:06:52.946 --> 00:06:55.260
and with my mother we
were reading this series

00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:56.580
and it was fictional

00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:58.620
but it had historical characters in it.

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:00.240
And so I googled this person because

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:01.740
of this fiction book I was reading,

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:04.050
Sarafina and The Twisted
Staff, something like that.

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:06.300
And it led me down this whole path

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:07.590
because I have a ranch

00:07:07.590 --> 00:07:08.760
and I love land.

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:10.639
I think we should leave most of it alone,

00:07:10.639 --> 00:07:11.910
again as much as possible.

00:07:11.910 --> 00:07:13.170
But, so he's known

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:15.090
as the father of landscape architecture

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:16.680
and he's also known

00:07:16.680 --> 00:07:17.513
- Central Park.

00:07:17.513 --> 00:07:18.600
- Yeah for Central Park.

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:19.647
Most of the major parks here.

00:07:19.647 --> 00:07:24.240
But also even more importantly
he, the Vanderbilt,

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:26.580
several wealthier people
back then were actually,

00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:28.830
he was the leader of why
we have national parks.

00:07:28.830 --> 00:07:30.450
Like it's, which is incredible

00:07:30.450 --> 00:07:32.190
because I think what's so important

00:07:32.190 --> 00:07:33.600
about Central Park that
you just brought up

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:37.470
is the thing I love
about learning is he was,

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:38.303
it was very cool

00:07:38.303 --> 00:07:40.050
because in the time it was
all for the wealthy, right?

00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:41.790
It was all for the higher ups.

00:07:41.790 --> 00:07:43.320
They got to enjoy these beautiful parks

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:44.310
or gardens or things.

00:07:44.310 --> 00:07:47.220
And his whole goal what,
and he won a contest

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:49.351
with this other French
architect to actually,

00:07:49.351 --> 00:07:52.146
to actually, I'm giving you
the whole book right now.

00:07:52.146 --> 00:07:53.760
But anyway, he won this contest

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:57.300
and it was so cool because he
wanted it to be for everyone.

00:07:57.300 --> 00:07:58.650
It was for all walks of people,

00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:00.270
all walks of life, all culture.

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:02.670
It was, which is pretty
progressive thinking,

00:08:02.670 --> 00:08:03.600
you know at the time

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:05.370
because most of the people paying for it

00:08:05.370 --> 00:08:06.747
were the wealthier people.

00:08:06.747 --> 00:08:07.580
- Yeah.

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:08.413
- So it was, it's just really cool.

00:08:08.413 --> 00:08:10.710
And I thought you can
learn a lot from someone

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:13.230
that you don't know, but
had such a well-lived life

00:08:13.230 --> 00:08:15.330
and had all these
different journeys to get

00:08:15.330 --> 00:08:18.095
to being this huge person for us.

00:08:18.095 --> 00:08:19.470
- Did you see the documentary about him?

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:20.430
- I haven't, but I've heard of it.

00:08:20.430 --> 00:08:22.410
But I haven't seen it yet, but I want to

00:08:22.410 --> 00:08:25.260
- You know, I made a movie
in Connecticut once and I

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:28.050
I used to run in those days
and I was running in a park

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:31.072
and I could tell by the
way the park was designed,

00:08:31.072 --> 00:08:34.022
I said Olmsted designed
this and I was right.

00:08:34.022 --> 00:08:35.103
Yeah, you can tell

00:08:35.103 --> 00:08:36.780
- It's very innovative.

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:39.030
I just think it's incredible
how people's minds think

00:08:39.030 --> 00:08:40.020
like that artistically.

00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:41.520
Mine does not.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:43.680
But now let's get to the
books from my audience.

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:45.630
Everyone brought something they cherish

00:08:45.630 --> 00:08:46.650
and want to pass on.

00:08:46.650 --> 00:08:48.180
Caitlin has a recommendation.

00:08:48.180 --> 00:08:49.710
Caitlin, what's your book?

00:08:49.710 --> 00:08:52.020
- I brought The Courage to be Disliked,

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:54.210
which when you talked about
your twenties being rough,

00:08:54.210 --> 00:08:55.770
I had some rough twenties.

00:08:55.770 --> 00:08:58.320
And this book really, it's
about freeing yourself

00:08:58.320 --> 00:08:59.610
from the judgment of others

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:01.920
and freeing yourself from
the judgment of yourself.

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:03.570
I'm going to be 35 this year,

00:09:03.570 --> 00:09:04.530
I'm getting married,

00:09:04.530 --> 00:09:05.910
I just started a new job,

00:09:05.910 --> 00:09:08.670
and I feel like this book
was a complete 180 for me

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:12.390
in order to find my own
happiness within myself.

00:09:12.390 --> 00:09:13.304
So I recommend

00:09:13.304 --> 00:09:15.150
- Congratulations on all
the freshness in your life.

00:09:15.150 --> 00:09:16.080
(audience cheers)

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:16.913
- Thank you.

00:09:16.913 --> 00:09:17.746
Thank you.

00:09:17.746 --> 00:09:18.840
- Yeah, but we also have Tamika,

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:19.953
so Tamika, what's your book?

00:09:19.953 --> 00:09:23.306
- It's The Secret Lives of
Church Ladies by Deesha Philyaw.

00:09:23.306 --> 00:09:26.430
And I'm a general manager
at a black-owned bookstore

00:09:26.430 --> 00:09:28.380
in Los Angeles called Reparations Club.

00:09:28.380 --> 00:09:31.170
And this was definitely
one of our best sellers.

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:32.925
It deals with the interior lives

00:09:32.925 --> 00:09:36.300
of black women and
femmes around the church.

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:38.490
And you don't have to
be an avid churchgoer

00:09:38.490 --> 00:09:39.660
in order to get into it

00:09:39.660 --> 00:09:41.895
because it really deals with the morality

00:09:41.895 --> 00:09:44.210
and some of the double
standards that exist

00:09:44.210 --> 00:09:47.925
when we apply them to our like real lives.

00:09:47.925 --> 00:09:50.220
There is some comedy in here,

00:09:50.220 --> 00:09:52.964
there are intergenerational
conversations happening,

00:09:52.964 --> 00:09:55.796
there is desire, there's queerness,

00:09:55.796 --> 00:09:58.740
and it's just an amazing,

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:00.375
it's a beautiful, beautiful book.

00:10:00.375 --> 00:10:03.510
It gives honor to the black church

00:10:03.510 --> 00:10:04.740
and the role that it plays

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:08.760
in the black community while
also interrogating it deeply

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:10.920
and how it sometimes contradicts

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:13.890
with the ways in which we
were, which we show up.

00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:16.170
And I deeply recommend it to everyone.

00:10:16.170 --> 00:10:17.003
- Wow.

00:10:17.003 --> 00:10:17.836
Yeah.

00:10:17.836 --> 00:10:19.003
Great job you guys.

00:10:19.003 --> 00:10:20.669
(audience claps)

00:10:20.669 --> 00:10:22.140
Well have either of you
read each other's books.

00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:23.040
Have either of you read those?

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:23.873
- No.

00:10:23.873 --> 00:10:26.130
- Are you willing to
switch and try it out?

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:27.090
I like it.

00:10:27.090 --> 00:10:27.923
I like it.

00:10:29.190 --> 00:10:30.376
Well, let's go ahead.

00:10:30.376 --> 00:10:32.400
They're swapped, they're
gonna switch books

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:34.920
and now everyone, it's
time to switch books.

00:10:34.920 --> 00:10:36.163
So turn to someone near you

00:10:36.163 --> 00:10:38.469
if you haven't read the
book that they're holding.

00:10:38.469 --> 00:10:40.800
Everybody has to exchange books.

00:10:40.800 --> 00:10:43.680
I don't care who it's with,
but read a different book that

00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:45.390
and even if you think you
won't find it interesting.

00:10:45.390 --> 00:10:46.680
I see Shel Silverstein up there.

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:47.739
That's amazing.

00:10:47.739 --> 00:10:48.572
(Kelly laughs)

00:10:48.572 --> 00:10:50.400
Is that where the sidewalk ends?

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:51.900
I love that y'all picked that.

00:10:52.890 --> 00:10:55.290
So much more reading for us all to do.

00:10:55.290 --> 00:10:58.500
And I love it to be part of
our Stranger Reads campaign

00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:01.080
and exchange your favorite
book titles with others.

00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:03.360
Just head to our show's
Insta Story right now.

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:04.660
You're welcome in advance.

